      "source_example_id": "e-9574",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve",
      "kp_id": "e-9574"
    },
    "q-127235": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127235",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123845/assets/95e6a455863f0b4cefe184cca9ad86feaeff9f0702808db2d151f5d6615a04b8.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i-y\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t},\\,t\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},2)$ to the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ Therefore, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14123845/assets/cc9d66f800a57f27673577cd3fd179b4cd6ff64585c5dc1a867317d8d592e485.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-y=-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})-(-\\frac{1}{t})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{2}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
